:: D04_Transit_Jordan [nobr]
<<HUD>>

<div class="prose-block">

<p>The walk home takes me through the campus-adjacent streets, tree-lined, old brownstones, a coffee shop on every corner. Saturday morning energy even though it's Monday, joggers, dog walkers, a couple pushing a stroller. Normal life humming along while I carry groceries and try to remember how to be a person.</p>

<p>A man holds the door at the corner bodega as I pass, thirties, suit, briefcase tucked under one arm even on a weekday morning off. He stops, waits, gestures with his free hand. "After you."</p>

<p>I'm not going into the bodega. I'm just walking past. But he's holding the door because I'm walking near it and he sees a woman and his reflex fires.</p>

<p>"Oh, I'm not... thanks though."</p>

<p>He nods, a little embarrassed, goes inside. The interaction lasted four seconds. Nothing happened. But something happened. Guys didn't hold doors for Marcus. The automatic chivalry, the pause and wait, the little half-bow, that's new.</p>

<p>The back of my neck prickles. His eyes followed me for a beat after I passed. Not threatening. Not aggressive. Just present, aware. He saw me.</p>

<p>I walk faster. The grocery bag bumps against my leg.</p>

</div>

<<ImagePlaceholder "A guy on the sidewalk with his hands raised, grinning, chasing her down" "float" "assets/characters/supporting/jordan-approach.png">>

<div class="prose-block">

<p>I'm two blocks from home when it happens.</p>

<p>Footsteps behind me, moving faster than walking pace, someone jogging to catch up. My shoulders tense. Every woman-safety article I accidentally read during yesterday's research marathon is screaming in the back of my skull. Don't have headphones in. Be aware of your surroundings. Walk with purpose.</p>

<p>"Hey! Excuse me?"</p>

<p>I turn. A guy, college age, tall, backward cap, easy grin. He's jogging the last few steps to close the gap, a little out of breath, hands up in a non-threatening gesture like he knows he just startled me.</p>

<p>"Sorry, I didn't mean to... I just had to come tell you. You're really pretty. That's all. Just wanted to say it."</p>

<p>My brain stalls.</p>

<p>A guy just ran down the sidewalk to tell me I'm pretty. This is a thing that happens to women. I knew this in theory. Marcus knew this in theory. But standing here with a bag of groceries and two razor nicks on my legs, being told by a stranger that I'm pretty, the theory evaporates and I'm left with nothing but a blank internal screen and a face that's probably doing something embarrassing.</p>

<p>"Oh. Um. Thanks?"</p>

<p>He laughs, a nice laugh, the kind that comes easy. "Sorry, was that weird? That was definitely weird."</p>

<p>"A little."</p>

<p>"My bad. I'm Jordan, by the way. In case you wanted to know the name of the weirdo who chased you down the street."</p>

<p>Despite everything, despite the absurdity, despite the fact that I was a man four days ago and this guy has no idea and would probably have some kind of existential crisis if he did, I almost laugh.</p>

</div>

<div class="choice-block">
	<<link '<span>😊 "Thanks. That&apos;s sweet."</span>' "D04_Jordan_Polite">>
		<<run window.statChange("con", 1)>>
	<</link>>
	<<link '<span>😤 "Don&apos;t chase people on the street."</span>' "D04_Jordan_Boundary">>
		<<run window.statChange("con", 2)>>
	<</link>>
	<<link '<span>😏 "I&apos;m Mia."</span>' "D04_Jordan_Number">>
		<<run window.statChange("cor", 3)>>
	<</link>>
</div>

:: D04_Jordan_Polite [nobr]
<<HUD>>

<div class="prose-block">

<p>"That's... thanks. That's sweet of you."</p>

<p>"I'm Jordan." He sticks out his hand like we're at a networking event, then realizes how formal that is and pulls it back, laughing at himself. "Sorry. I don't actually do this ever. You can probably tell."</p>

<p>"I can tell."</p>

<p>"Okay, I'm going to go die of embarrassment now. Have a great day." He finger-guns, actually finger-guns, then catches himself doing it and winces. "I'm making it worse, aren't I."</p>

<p>"A little."</p>

<p>He backs away, grinning, both hands in the air like he's surrendering. "I'm leaving. You're pretty. That's all. Bye."</p>

<p>He jogs back the way he came. I watch him go. At the corner he looks back, gives a little wave, and disappears.</p>

<p>I stand there holding my groceries. The paper bag crinkles in my grip. My cheeks are hot, which is a physiological betrayal I didn't consent to. Marcus would have laughed this off, maybe felt secondhand embarrassment for the guy. But I'm not Marcus standing on this sidewalk. A cute guy just told me I'm pretty, and my face is red, and this is apparently my life now.</p>

</div>

<<run window.statChange("fem", 1)>>
<<set $d04MetJordan to true>>

<div class="choice-block">
	<<link '<span>🚪 Keep walking</span>' "Outside">><</link>>
</div>

:: D04_Jordan_Boundary [nobr]
<<HUD>>

<div class="prose-block">

<p>"I don't know you. Don't run up on people on the street."</p>

<p>The grin drops. Not anger, not defensiveness, genuine recalibration. His hands come up higher, more deliberate.</p>

<p>"You're absolutely right. I'm sorry. That was thoughtless."</p>

<p>"It's not a good feeling, having someone chase you."</p>

<p>He flinches. The word "chase" lands and I can see him replaying the last thirty seconds from my side of it. "Yeah. Shit, yeah, I can see how that... I'm really sorry. That won't happen again."</p>

<p>He takes a step back, then another, his body language shifting from confident casual to small and sorry. "I'll go. I'm sorry."</p>

<p>He turns and walks away, not jogging, walking slowly, shoulders hunched slightly, the confidence knocked out of him. At the corner he glances back once, not smiling, then rounds it and is gone.</p>

<p>I breathe. My hands are shaking around the grocery bag. The shake isn't fear. It's the adrenaline dump of confrontation, the chemical aftermath of standing your ground. Marcus would have said "no worries, man" and moved on. Mia said "don't do that" and meant it.</p>

<p>My hands shake for two blocks. I let them.</p>

</div>

<<run window.statChange("fem", 1)>>
<<set $d04MetJordan to true>>

<div class="choice-block">
	<<link '<span>🏠 Get home. Lock the door.</span>' "Outside">><</link>>
</div>

:: D04_Jordan_Number [nobr]
<<HUD>>

<div class="prose-block">

<p>The word thanks is already forming on my lips but something else comes out instead. Something reckless and terrifying and alive.</p>

<p>"I'm Mia."</p>

<p>His face does a thing. The grin broadens, the eyes widen, the whole expression reorganizes around a new piece of information. He's genuinely surprised I engaged. Maybe he expected the brush-off. Maybe he does this all the time and usually gets rejected. Maybe this is the first time he's ever chased a girl down the street and can't believe it worked.</p>

<p>"Mia. I love that name." He says it like he means it, not like a line. "I'm Jordan. Can I... would it be weird if I texted you sometime?"</p>

<p>I pull my phone from my criminally shallow pocket. My fingers are buzzing. "What's your number?"</p>

<p>He recites it. I punch it in, my thumbs clumsy, having to delete and retype because the adrenaline is making my fine motor skills drunk. I send him a text, just a period, a dot, the smallest possible footprint of contact.</p>

<p>His pocket buzzes. He pulls out his phone, sees the dot, grins.</p>

<p>"Mia with the period. I'll remember that."</p>

<p>"Don't be weird about it."</p>

<p>"I literally chased you down the street. That ship has sailed." He laughs. I laugh. The laughter breaks something open, and for three seconds I'm not a person in crisis, not a body in transition, not a question mark in jeans. I'm just a girl laughing on a sidewalk with a guy who has a nice smile and no idea what he's gotten himself into.</p>

<p>"I'll text you," he says. "Promise."</p>

<p>"You better."</p>

<p>Where did that come from? Who said that? That was not Marcus. Marcus didn't "you better" anyone. That was Mia, and Mia apparently has a flirtatious streak that showed up uninvited.</p>

<p>He jogs off. I watch him go, this tall ridiculous person who ran after me on the street because he thought I was pretty. When he rounds the corner I look down at my phone. His name is in my contacts now, Jordan, the first male contact I've saved as Mia.</p>

<p>I walk home. The grocery bag bumps my leg. My phone feels heavier in my pocket, like it's gained mass from the new number.</p>

</div>

<<run window.statChange("fem", 2)>>
<<set $jordan.met to true>>
<<set $d04MetJordan to true>>

